verdict.reasoning:
I read the code in the clone at ~/github_projects/encoder-collision-corpus/CreateAPI__CreateAPI, which is at the frozen revision 7d0a70a8.

The id function really does merge distinct names. makeProperty (Generator+Schemas.swift:613) calls makePropertyName(rename(key:)), which leads to PropertyName(processing:) and then String.process(isProperty: true) (Naming.swift:134). process does four things:
- removes "'";
- splits on CharacterSet.alphanumerics.inverted (badCharacters, Naming.swift:225);
- lowercases the parts only if every part longer than one character has no lowercase letter;
- drops empty parts and joins them in camelCase.

So "first_name" splits into ["first","name"] and becomes "firstName". "firstName" splits into ["firstName"], is not lowercased because it has a lowercase letter, and stays "firstName". The function is a split, filter and camelCase pass that discards separators. It is not a replacingOccurrences chain. The pair is realistic: an OpenAPI object can carry both a snake_case key and a camelCase key.

The refutation is that something does dedupe. In makeObject (line 289), the result of makeInlineProperties goes through `.removingDuplicates(by: \.name)` (Helpers.swift:107). That function walks the properties with a Set<PropertyName> and keeps the first one for each name. makeAllOf (line 492) and Generator+Paths.swift:408 do the same. So the generated Swift never declares the identifier twice: each surviving id gets exactly one property, and the output compiles. The handling is lossy, though. The second key is dropped silently, with no warning, uniquifier or `_2` suffix, so the generated type cannot decode or encode that JSON key. That is a real silent data-loss defect, and it is worth recording separately as the drop-on-collision variant. But this task counts a site as real only if nothing dedupes and the output ends up merging or redeclaring entities per element. Here a Set-keyed dedupe runs between the id function and the emitted declarations, and it is deliberate: the comment says Swifty bool renames create duplicates. So by the stated criterion this is not a #674 site.

Secondary point: the domain is JSON property keys inside a schema, not type, module or file names. The emitter is generated Swift source, with one property per element after the dedupe.
